    "narrative": "The core of today\u2019s offensive strategy relies heavily on exploiting favorable matchups against high-xERA starters. **James Wood** (76.28) is our highest-rated start, buoyed by a premier matchup against Mitch Bratt (5.90 xERA) and the premium leadoff spot. We also expect a massive output from **Austin Riley** (73.69), who benefits from a 16% park factor boost and a soft matchup against Brandon Young. **Brice Turang** (72.70) remains essential for our SB-heavy build; his spot in the three-hole against the vulnerable Ryan Feltner provides both volume and a high probability of stolen base opportunities. While **Corbin Carroll** (64.15) faces a tougher arm in Foster Griffin, his ability to manipulate the basepaths against a pitcher with a slow delivery keeps his floor elevated for our stolen base-starved categories.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Isaac Paredes** (52.31). He finished just behind **Josh Naylor** (52.92), but the optimizer favored the latter due to the perceived impact of **Josh Naylor** hitting in the heart of the order. Conversely, **Isaac Paredes** suffered from a slight park factor penalty and was ultimately squeezed out of a congested infield. We are monitoring **Gabriel Moreno**, **Max Muncy**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Tyler Soderstrom** closely as their starting positions remain pending; any shift in their batting order, particularly for **Jose Caballero** or **Travis Bazzana**, could necessitate a pivot if they drop too far down the lineup, as we are prioritizing runs and stolen base volume today. \n\nWe are proceeding with caution regarding **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll** due to their matchup against Foster Griffin. While the projections are favorable, facing a left-handed starter remains a dynamic platoon concern. We have depth ready on the bench in **Seiya Suzuki** and **Mickey Moniak**, though both face stiff competition today against elite arms like Paul Skenes and Robert Gasser. **Zach McKinstry** (50.61) rounds out the outfield as our final starter; he\u2019s essentially a volume play, as his efficiency is marginal, but he serves as a placeholder to ensure we maintain our daily game cap utilization without sacrificing significant category production.",
